{{smaller|degrees of excellence in the hulls of ships, the figure 1 being added to express excellence of equipment, such as masts and rigging in sailing-ships, or boilers and engines in steamers. The broad A in the British Lloyd’s indicates a ship built of iron. In the American Register, the annexed figures do not refer to the equipment.—Hence, in commerce, ''A1'' is used to denote the highest mercantile credit; and colloquially ''A1'', or in the United States ''A No.'' 1, is an adjective of commendation, like ''first-class'', ''first-rate'': as, an ''A1'' speaker.}}
